//// rakun-web — THE CHAIN. One ordered list of entries between the socket and
//// the route handler, and the response surface a filter composes with.
////
//// WHY THERE IS ONE. The 1.0.6 draft asked for Spring filters and the 1.0.7
//// draft asked for a Next-style `middleware.bp`. Read side by side they
//// describe the same thing: something that sees a request before the handler,
//// may answer it instead, may change it, and sees the response on the way out.
//// Two pipelines would mean two orderings, a CORS header set in one and
//// overwritten in the other, and no answer to "does my filter run before the
//// middleware". `#[filter]` and `#[middleware]` are two ENTRY POINTS into this
//// one chain, at two default orders, and `convention.bp` says which is which.
////
//// WHERE IT HOOKS IN. Front 04's `dispatch_http/5` calls `rakun_chain:run/6`
//// when this module is in the build and the handler directly when it is not
//// (`modules/rakun/src/sidecars/rakun_runtime.erl`). That branch is the entire
//// integration surface; an application without rakun-web pays one
//// `function_exported/3` per request and gets front 04's behaviour exactly.
////
//// WHAT THE CHAIN'S REQUEST VALUE IS, AND WHY IT IS NOT `Request`. rakun's
//// `Request` is a host-supplied `behavior`, and a method on one does not
//// dispatch on the erlang row: `req.header("origin")` lowers to a map field
//// read of `header` followed by a call, which is `{badkey,header}` /
//// `{badfun, …}` — the same defect that keeps `test/server_test.bp:74,80` red
//// in the core. A chain that cannot read a header cannot do CORS, so the chain
//// carries its own RECORD, `WebRequest`, built from the same scalars the
//// dispatcher already has. Record methods dispatch on both rows. The deviation
//// from the spec's `Filter.handle(self, req: Request, chain: Chain)` is one
//// type name and it is written down in `AGENTS.md` beside the measurement.

import {Response} from "rakun";
import {sub} from "rakun";
import {headerLookup, headerNames, headerPresent} from "rakun";
import {pattern: {parsePattern, matchPattern}} from "routing";

// ── the host cells ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Both forms, always. The registries and the four per-request accumulators are
// the host's; the order band, the sentinel rule, the replace-by-name rule, the
// `Vary` union and every refusal are below, in botopink, compiled twice.

